BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ea14f4280fca9c66@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260802T111914Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260225
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Proctor v. Sappi North America
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE  APPELLATE DIVISION \nWORKERS’ COMPENSATION B
 OARD  Case No. App. Div. 25-0008 \n  Decision No. 26-01 \n \n \nPHILIP PRO
 CTOR \n(Appellant) \n \nv. \nSAPPI NORTH AMERICA\, INC. \n(Appellee/Self-I
 nsured) \n \n \nArgued: September 23\, 2025 \nDecided: February 25\, 2026 
 \n \nPanel Members:  Administrative Law Judges Chabot\, Sands\, and Smith 
 \nBy:  Administrative Law Judge Sands \n \n[¶1]  Philip Proctor appeals f
 rom a decision of a Workers’ Compensation \nBoard administrative law jud
 ge ( Stovall\, ALJ ) denying \, in part\, his Petition for \nPenalties. Th
 rough his Petition\, Mr. Proctor sought to establish that  Sappi North \nA
 merica\, Inc. \, violated the Workers’ Compensation Act by taking an off
 set for \npension benefits distributed by Sappi and subsequently placed by
  Mr. Proctor into \nan Individual Retirement Account (IRA). See 39-A M.R.S
 .A . § 221. 1 The ALJ \ndetermined that Sappi is entitled to take the off
 set and declined to impose penalties \nbeyond those associated with late p
 ayments under 39-A M.R.S.A. § 205(3). We find \nno error and affirm the d
 ecision.   \n \n \n  1  Because this appeal pertains  to whether Sappi has
  a right under section 221 of the Workers’ \nCompensation Act to take an
  offset for pension benefits paid\, and does not address the imposition or
  denial \nof penalties\, the Appellate Division\, rather than the Superior
  Court\, has jurisdiction to entertain this appeal. \nSee 39-A M.R.S.A §
 § 318\, 32-B\, and 360. \n \n2 \n \nI.  BACKGROUND \n[¶2]  Mr. Proctor s
 ustained a left-hand crush injury on April 28\, 2010\, while \nworking for
  Sappi . Ultimately\, he was  unable to return to work\, and Sappi has \nv
 oluntarily paid him indemnity benefits since.   \n[¶3]  Through his emplo
 yment at Sappi\, Mr. Proctor participated in a defined \nbenefit pension p
 lan. Pursuant to the plan’s terms\, benefits must be distributed when \n
 individuals no longer employed by Sappi reach age 65 . Notice is sent to t
 hose \nindividuals in advance with three options for distribution. The def
 ault distribution\, \nper t\nhttps://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/appella
 te/2026decisions/26-01_Proctor_v._Sappi_%20N._America_2-25-26.pdf
URL:https://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/appellate/2026decisions/26-01_Pr
 octor_v._Sappi_%20N._America_2-25-26.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f180f9edcb8236e2@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260802T111854Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260802
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Flexibility Requested for Federal Employees a
 nd other Impacted Persons
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE  \nDEPA RTMENT O F P R OFES SIO NAL  & FINA NCI
 AL  RE GUL ATIO N  \nBUREAU OF  INSU RAN CE  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nOffice
  Location: 76 Northern Avenue\, Gardiner\, Maine 04345 \nMailing Address: 
 34 State House Station\, Augusta\, Maine 04333 \nwww.maine.gov/pfr/insuran
 ce \nPhone: (207) 624-8475  TTY: Please Call Maine Relay 711 Consumer Assi
 stance: (800) 300-5000 Fax: (207) 624-8599 \n \nJanet T. Mills \nGovernor 
 \nRobert L. Carey \nSuperintendent \nJoan F. Cohen \nCommissioner \nBullet
 in 488 \nFlexibility Requested for Federal Employees and other Impacted Pe
 rsons \nMany Maine residents are experiencing financial hardship due to th
 e continuing federal \ngovernment shutdown. In response to this disruption
 \, the Bureau requests that all Maine licensed \ninsurance companies be fl
 exible and proactive in working with policyholders who are experiencing \n
 temporary financial hardship due to the shutdown to keep essential insuran
 ce policies in place. \nThis includes federal employees and other persons 
 whose pay has been directly impacted\, such as \npersons whose employment 
 relies upon federal grants and contracts which have been affected by \nthe
  shutdown. Specifically\, the Bureau requests that all insurance companies
  provide these affected \npolicyholders with at least a 60 -day grace peri
 od to pay insurance premiums or other flexible \npayment options so that i
 nsurance policies are not cancelled for nonpayment of premium and \npenalt
 ies are not assessed during this challenging time.  \nThe requested grace 
 period and flexible payment options are intended to be applied to \npremiu
 ms due after the initial premium has been made to secure coverage. They ar
 e not intended \nto change the terms of the issued policy or to be conside
 red a forgiveness of the premium. This \nrequest is directed to all admitt
 ed and non -admitted insurance companies that provide any \ninsurance cove
 rage in Maine including life\, health\, auto\, property\, casualty\, and o
 ther types of \ninsurance. The Bureau  encourages all carriers  \nhttps://
 www.maine.gov/pfr/insurance/sites/maine.gov.pfr.insurance/files/inline-fil
 es/Bulletin%20488.pdf
URL:https://www.maine.gov/pfr/insurance/sites/maine.gov.pfr.insurance/file
 s/inline-files/Bulletin%20488.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c06d1d15e6d83536@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260802T111844Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260426
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] John G. Pappas v. Stephen H. Leask d/b/a Sout
 hgate Restaurant
DESCRIPTION:1  \nSTATE OF MAINE BUSINESS AND CONSUMER COURT  Cumberland\, 
 ss. Location:  Portland  Docket No.:  BCD-CV-15-07   JOHN G. PAPPAS\,  Pla
 intiff\,  v.  STEVEN H.  LEASK  d/b/a Southgate Restaurant\,  Defendant.  
   \n) ) ) ) ) ) ) ) ) ) )  \n       \nDECISION AND JUDGMENT  Plaintiff Joh
 n Pappas has brought this action against Defendant Steven Leask\, doing bu
 siness as Southgate Restaurant\, seeking damages for employment discrimina
 tion in violation of the Maine Human Rights Act.  A trial was held Februar
 y 24\, 2016 through February 26\, 2016.  By agreement of the parties\, wit
 nesses other than the parties were sequestered throughout the trial.  Afte
 r the trial\, the parties submitted proposed findings of fact and conclusi
 ons of law.    Based on the entire record\, the court hereby adopts the fi
 ndings and conclusions set forth below\, and grants judgment to Defendant.
  1.   Steven H. Leask has worked as a machinist for Bath Iron Works (BIW) 
 for many years.  For many years\, the Southgate Restaurant has been a loca
 lly owned restaurant serving breakfast and lunch at its location in Bath\,
  near the BIW facility.  Mr. Leask was a frequent diner at Southgate befor
 e 2006.  In that year\, after learning \n2  \nthat the owners were plannin
 g to close the restaurant\, Mr. Leask purchased it in his own name.   2. H
 e has had no training or experience in operating any business\, including 
 a restaurant\, and he did not give up his job at BIW after making the purc
 hase—his purpose in buying Southgate was simply to keep the restaurant o
 pen.   The purchase was a turnkey transaction\, and Mr. Leask changed very
  little after the purchase.  Mr. Leask runs the restaurant very informally
 .  His involvement in day-to-day restaurant operations has been limited to
  coming in around the 2 p.m. closing time\, to tally the restaurant’s re
 ceipts for the day.  3. Responsibility for the daily operation of the rest
 aurant rests mainly with the restaurant manager\, Doreen Beaulieu\, who al
 so is Mr. Leask’s domestic partner.   The othe\nhttps://www.courts.maine
 .gov/courts/bcd/cv-orders/CV-15-07/CV-15-07%20Pappas%20v%20Leask%20Decisio
 n.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/bcd/cv-orders/CV-15-07/CV-15-07%20
 Pappas%20v%20Leask%20Decision.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
